<tr><th>Code</th><th>Valid?</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = 97;</td><td>valid</td><td>prints a</td></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = 0xFACE;</td><td>valid</td><td>hex form; value 64,206; corresponding character assigned. (0xFACE = 64206, within 0–65535.) He says “0 x of f” / FACE on the board.</td></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = 0777;</td><td>valid</td><td>octal; value 511 (or “511 or something”); corresponding character assigned</td></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = 65535;</td><td>valid</td><td>max char</td></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = 65536;</td><td>invalid</td><td>out of range</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th>Escape</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>\n</td><td>new line</td></tr>
<tr><td>\t</td><td>horizontal tab</td></tr>
<tr><td>\r</td><td>carriage return — he explains: move to the first cell of the next line (like hitting return while typing)</td></tr>
<tr><td>\b</td><td>backspace (not blank space). Keyboard key that moves one cell back and removes a character if present</td></tr>
<tr><td>\f</td><td>form feed (“maybe replacement; I’m not sure — check C books; as a programmer not required to worry”)</td></tr>
<tr><td>\&#x27;</td><td>single quote as a symbol</td></tr>
<tr><td>\&quot;</td><td>double quote as a symbol</td></tr>
<tr><td>\\</td><td>backslash as a symbol</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th>Code</th><th>Valid?</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = 65536;</td><td>invalid</td><td>out of range (max 65535)</td></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = 0xBeer;</td><td>invalid</td><td>hex digits are 0–9, A–F. `R` is not hex. (0xBEEF would be fine; beer is not)</td></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = \uface;</td><td>invalid</td><td>single quotes missing. Unicode form must be &#x27;\uface&#x27;</td></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = &#x27;\ubeef&#x27;;</td><td>valid</td><td>BEEF is four hex digits; quotes present</td></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = &#x27;\m&#x27;;</td><td>invalid</td><td>illegal escape character</td></tr>
<tr><td>char ch = &#x27;\iface&#x27;;</td><td>invalid</td><td>`\i` is not an escape. (FACE as Unicode would be valid if written &#x27;\uface&#x27;)</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>char</th><th>short</th></tr>
<tr><td>Size</td><td>2 bytes</td><td>2 bytes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Signed?</td><td>unsigned (in the sense he teaches: no negative, all 16 bits are value)</td><td>signed (1 bit sign, 15 bits value)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Range</td><td>0 to 65,535</td><td>−32,768 to 32,767</td></tr>
</table>
</body></html>
